{"builder":"Goal: make `packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js` accept several files, and open one pull request that meets every acceptance line of https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104.\n\nAcceptance lines (each must hold):\n1. packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...] checks every file named in order and prints <file>:<line>: <error> for each bad row, as for one file before\n2. A file named - is read from stdin and reported as -; a second - is a usage error (exit 2)\n3. Exit 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, 2 if no file is named or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported)\n4. lint(text) in src/index.js is unchanged\n5. node:test tests cover two clean files, one bad among several, stdin via - (clean and bad), a missing file among good ones, two - arguments and no arguments; the package README documents the usage\n6. The change is one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test whose body says Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104, and npm test and the test workflow are green at its head\n\nSteps:\n1. Read `packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js`, `packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js`, `packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js`, `packages/jsonl-lint/test/lint.test.js`, `packages/jsonl-lint/README.md` and `.github/workflows/test.yml`. The CLI today takes one file, prints `<file>:<line>: <error>` (newlines in the error replaced by a space), exits 0/1, and exits 2 with a stderr message on no argument or an unreadable file.\n2. Rewrite only the CLI in `packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js`:\n   - Take all of `process.argv.slice(2)` as files. With none, write a usage line (e.g. `usage: jsonl-lint <file> [<file> ...]`) to stderr and exit 2.\n   - Before reading anything, if `-` appears more than once, write a usage error to stderr and exit 2.\n   - Process files in the order given. For `-`, read all of stdin (e.g. `readFileSync(0, 'utf8')`) and use `-` as the name in output. For others, `readFileSync(file, 'utf8')`; on failure write `jsonl-lint: cannot read <file>: <message>` to stderr, remember the failure, and carry on with the next file.\n   - For each readable file, call `lint(text)` and print `<name>:<line>: <error>` lines exactly as the one-file version did.\n   - Exit 2 if any file couldn't be read; otherwise 1 if any row in any file was bad; otherwise 0. Keep zero runtime dependencies and plain ES modules.\n3. Do not modify `lint` in `packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js`. Best not to touch that file at all.\n4. Extend `packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js` (node:test, spawning the CLI with `child_process.spawnSync`/`execFile` and temp files) with tests for: two clean files (exit 0, no stdout); one bad file among several clean ones (exit 1, only the bad file's `<file>:<line>:` lines, files in order); stdin via `-` clean (exit 0) and bad (exit 1, lines start with `-:`); a missing file among good ones (exit 2, stderr names the missing file, a bad row in another given file is still reported on stdout); two `-` arguments (exit 2); no arguments (exit 2, usage on stderr). Keep the existing tests passing.\n5. Update `packages/jsonl-lint/README.md` to document the usage: several files, `-` for stdin (at most once), output format, and the exit codes 0/1/2.\n6. Run `npm test` at the repository root and make it pass.\n7. Commit, push and open one pull request on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test. The PR body must contain the line `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`.\n8. Wait for the test workflow (`.github/workflows/test.yml`) on the PR head to finish (`gh pr checks <pr> --watch`). If it fails, fix, push and wait again until it is green.","verifier":"Check out the pull request head for https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104 and run `npm ci` or `npm install` at the repository root if needed. Make a scratch directory outside the repo (e.g. `T=$(mktemp -d)`) with: `$T/good1.jsonl` = `{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}\\n`, `$T/good2.jsonl` = `[1,2]\\n`, `$T/bad.jsonl` = `{\"a\":1}\\n{bad\\n3\\n` (row 2 bad). Let `CLI=packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js`. For each check, record the command, its stdout, stderr and exit status. There is exactly one check per acceptance line, in order.\n\nCheck 1 — \"packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...] checks every file named in order and prints <file>:<line>: <error> for each bad row, as for one file before\"\nRun `node $CLI $T/good1.jsonl $T/bad.jsonl $T/good2.jsonl`, then `cp $T/bad.jsonl $T/bad2.jsonl` and `node $CLI $T/bad.jsonl $T/bad2.jsonl`, and also `node $CLI $T/bad.jsonl` alone.\nPASS: every file named is checked; each bad row appears as a line `<file>:<line>: <error>` with the file as named and the right line number (`$T/bad.jsonl:2: ...`); in the two-bad-file run, all of bad.jsonl's lines come before bad2.jsonl's; the single-file run still prints `<file>:<line>: <error>` lines for bad rows.\nFAIL: any file named is skipped, a bad row is missing or shows a wrong file name or line, output is out of argument order, or the single-file format is not `<file>:<line>: <error>`.\n\nCheck 2 — \"A file named - is read from stdin and reported as -; a second - is a usage error (exit 2)\"\nRun `node $CLI - < $T/bad.jsonl`, `node $CLI $T/good1.jsonl - < $T/bad.jsonl`, and `node $CLI - - < $T/good1.jsonl`.\nPASS: the first two runs report the bad row as `-:2: <error>` (stdin read, named `-`); the `- -` run exits with status 2.\nFAIL: stdin is not read for `-`, the bad row is reported under any name other than `-`, or `- -` exits with a status other than 2.\n\nCheck 3 — \"Exit 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, 2 if no file is named or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported)\"\nRun: `node $CLI $T/good1.jsonl $T/good2.jsonl` (expect 0); `node $CLI $T/good1.jsonl $T/bad.jsonl` (expect 1); `node $CLI` (expect 2); `node $CLI $T/good1.jsonl $T/missing.jsonl $T/bad.jsonl` (expect 2).\nPASS: the exit statuses are 0, 1, 2 and 2 respectively; in the last run stderr holds an error message about the unreadable file, and stdout still reports `$T/bad.jsonl:2: ...` (the file after the missing one was still checked and reported).\nFAIL: any exit status differs, the unreadable-file error is not on stderr, or the bad row of `$T/bad.jsonl` is not reported in the run with a missing file.\n\nCheck 4 — \"lint(text) in src/index.js is unchanged\"\nRun `git diff $(git merge-base HEAD origin/main) HEAD -- packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js` and inspect it; also run `node -e \"import('./packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js').then(m=>console.log(JSON.stringify(m.lint('{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n{bad\\n\\n3'))))\"` and compare with the same command run at the base commit (`git stash`-free: e.g. `git worktree add $T/base $(git merge-base HEAD origin/main)` and run it there).\nPASS: the diff makes no change to the `lint` function's code (its body and signature are identical to the base), and the two outputs are identical.\nFAIL: the `lint` function's code is changed in any way, or the outputs differ.\n\nCheck 5 — \"node:test tests cover two clean files, one bad among several, stdin via - (clean and bad), a missing file among good ones, two - arguments and no arguments; the package README documents the usage\"\nRead the test files under `packages/jsonl-lint/test/` and run `npm test --workspace packages/jsonl-lint` (or `cd packages/jsonl-lint && node --test`). Read `packages/jsonl-lint/README.md`.\nPASS: the tests use `node:test` and include cases for each of: two clean files; one bad file among several; stdin via `-` with clean input; stdin via `-` with bad input; a missing file among good ones; two `-` arguments; no arguments — and those tests run (they appear in the test run output); and the README describes invoking the CLI with several files and with `-` for stdin.\nFAIL: any of the seven listed cases has no test